-// Chapter 29 checkpoint 4: decomposed confidence and one application of the split-sum weight.
+// Chapter 29 checkpoint 5: bounded one-bounce screen-space diffuse indirect lighting.
 //
-// A screen-space hit is evidence, not certainty. The classified trace becomes six independently bounded confidence
-// factors - validity, screen-edge proximity, distance travelled, thickness agreement, roughness, and how far the ray
-// points back toward the camera - and they stay decomposed so a learner can see which assumption failed. Their
-// product is a fade weight, not a probability. Screen radiance and environment radiance are then combined with the
-// weights confidence and 1 - confidence, which sum to exactly one, so the incoming radiance is a partition of a
-// single quantity instead of a sum of two guesses. The split-sum weight F0 * A + B is applied once, after that
-// combination, so a partial screen answer can never be shaded twice.
+// The same screen evidence that answers a mirror ray can answer a diffuse one. This checkpoint traces a small
+// deterministic set of cosine-weighted hemisphere samples from the shaded point, reuses the radiance that already
+// left each hit surface this frame, and falls back to the environment for every sample the screen could not answer,
+// because a screen-space miss is unmeasured light rather than zero light. The estimator keeps its two factors of pi
+// separate: the cosine density makes cos / pdf exactly pi, while the albedo / pi belongs to the Lambertian surface,
+// so the two must never be cancelled by hand. Every sample is clamped to a maximum radiance and the hit, miss, and
+// clamp counts are published, so the bias that bound introduces stays visible instead of hidden.
